--[[
	THE ASSOCIATION ARCHIVE: lore entries (kept across lives). { id, category, title, text, secret }
	Each says what it is: [CANON] (the manhwa), or [GAME] (this game's own rule or number).
	Facts follow docs/BITLEVELING_CANON.md; uncertain ones are worded as uncertain.

	The bestiary's entries (one a family of monsters, from Hunter/Bestiary.luau) say whether the
	family is a canon TYPE ([CANON, type]: in this game's own designs, never a canon named
	individual) or this game's own ([GAME] ... ORIGINAL). They file under BESTIARY, below.
]]

-- (the codex category the bestiary files under: Spec's codex.categories. "gates" until a
-- "bestiary" category is added there, then change this one word)
local BESTIARY = "gates"

return {
	-- ── Gates and Dungeons ──
	{ id = "lore_gates", category = "gates", title = "Gates", text = "[CANON] About ten years before the story begins, Gates started opening all over the world: tears in the air, leading to dungeons full of monsters. Every Gate has a rank, E, D, C, B, A or S, from the strength of the mana it gives off. The Hunter Association measures it and sells the right to raid it.\n\n[GAME] Gates here are not a menu. The world makes its own, in streets, stations, forests and farms, and runs each one through its life: it opens, someone notices (or doesn't), the Association responds, a raid goes in, and it's cleared or it breaks." },
	{ id = "lore_ranks", category = "gates", title = "Ranks", text = "[CANON] Hunters and Gates share six ranks: E, D, C, B, A, S. A Hunter's rank is measured when they awaken and, almost always, it stays the same for life. S-rank Hunters are very rare: in canon, Korea recognised only ten in the whole story.\n\n[CANON] There's no colour code for Gate ranks. Ordinary Gates look blue. Only a Red Gate is named for its colour, and it turns red after the raid has gone in.\n\n[GAME] Rank mismatch is real here. An E-rank Hunter in front of an A-rank monster will almost certainly die, and the odds on the card will tell you so." },
	{ id = "lore_breaks", category = "gates", title = "Dungeon Breaks", text = "[CANON] A Gate that isn't cleared in time breaks: its monsters come out into the world. The figure people cite is about seven days, but in canon an S-rank Gate in Tokyo broke after about three. Treat seven days as typical, not a promise.\n\n[GAME] A Gate nobody finds (deep in a forest, up a mountain) is the most dangerous kind: it's discovered by the people its monsters reach first." },
	{ id = "lore_crystals", category = "gates", title = "Mana Crystals", text = "[CANON] Monsters carry mana crystals, and dungeons hold deposits of them. They're a Hunter's main income, an energy source and a crafting material. Electronics fail inside dungeons, so mining crews dig by hand with pickaxes. Guilds employ porters and miners; rare loot goes to auction.\n\n[CANON, likely] A Gate closes about an hour after its boss dies, so collection teams work fast." },
	{ id = "lore_red", category = "gates", title = "Red Gates", text = "[CANON] A Red Gate looks ordinary until the raid is inside. Then it turns red and the way out disappears. Nobody can get in, nobody can get out, until the boss dies. The reading taken before entry is wrong: in canon's Red Gate it said C, and what was inside was far worse.\n\n[CANON, likely] Time runs differently inside: about a day for every hour outside.\n\n[GAME] Red Gates are rare here. When one happens near you, you'll know." },
	{ id = "lore_double", category = "gates", title = "Double Dungeons", text = "[CANON] A Double Dungeon is a second, hidden dungeon inside an ordinary one. The only canon case, the Cartenon Temple inside a D-rank Gate, killed most of the raid party that found it.\n\n[GAME] Double Dungeons here are vanishingly rare, never repeatable, and never the Cartenon Temple. What chose Sung Jinwoo chose him; nothing in a Double Dungeon chooses you." },
	{ id = "lore_rights", category = "gates", title = "Raid Rights", text = "[CANON, likely] The Association detects and measures Gates, then sells the right to raid them to guilds and parties. Big guilds can be required to take emergency Gates, in return for tax breaks.\n\n[GAME] Going into a Gate you don't hold the rights to is against the law here. People still do it." },

	-- ── Hunters ──
	{ id = "lore_awakening", category = "hunters", title = "Awakening", text = "[CANON] After the Gates came, some people awakened: they gained mana and abilities, and became Hunters. It seems random and can happen at any age. Awakened Hunters register with their country's Association, are tested, and receive a licence.\n\n[GAME] Most lives here never awaken. The odds by age, the rank you awaken at and the class are this game's numbers, because canon gives none." },
	{ id = "lore_classes", category = "hunters", title = "Hunter Classes", text = "[CANON] Hunters are described by class: Fighters, Mages, Assassins, Tankers, Rangers and Healers. A raid party needs all of them: Tankers in front, Healers behind, everyone else somewhere in between.\n\n[GAME] The class you awaken with leans your stats; it doesn't stop you from becoming anything else." },
	{ id = "lore_reawakening", category = "hunters", title = "Reawakening", text = "[CANON] Rank is normally fixed. A reawakening, a second awakening that raises a Hunter's rank, is extremely rare, rare enough that when an E-rank came back from a reassessment as an S-rank, the Association assumed that's what it was.\n\n[GAME] A life here can reawaken, once, by a very long chance." },
	{ id = "lore_fear", category = "hunters", title = "Fear", text = "[GAME] Hunters freeze in front of things that are stronger than them. Here, Willpower is how much fear a mind can carry, and Composure is how much is left. A shaken Hunter loses clashes they should win." },
	{ id = "lore_system", category = "hunters", title = "The System", text = "[CANON] One Hunter had a System: a game-like window only he could see, that let him grow stronger without limit. It was built to raise a vessel for the Shadow Monarch, and it chose Sung Jinwoo.\n\n[GAME] Nobody else has one. Not you either. Hunters here get stronger the way real people do: training, experience, and the limits of their rank." },

	-- ── Associations and Guilds ──
	{ id = "lore_kha", category = "orgs", title = "The Korean Hunters Association", text = "[CANON] Korea's Association licenses Hunters, measures Gates and sells raid rights. Its chairman through the story is Go Gunhee. Woo Jinchul, an A-rank, heads the Surveillance Team that polices Hunter crime, and later leads the Association." },
	{ id = "lore_guilds", category = "orgs", title = "Korea's Guilds", text = "[CANON] The Hunters Guild is the largest, the only one with two S-ranks: master Choi Jong-In, a fire mage, and vice-master Cha Hae-In, a swordswoman. The White Tiger Guild's master is Baek Yoonho, an S-rank who fights in the shape of a beast. The Fame Guild's Ma Dongwook, an S-rank Tanker, holds the Honam region. Late in the story Sung Jinwoo founds the Ahjin Guild, with Yoo Jinho as vice-master.\n\n[GAME] The guilds you'll actually work for are small and mid-size ones: this game's own." },
	{ id = "lore_abroad", category = "orgs", title = "Abroad", text = "[CANON] In the United States, the Hunter Bureau is a federal agency; it hosts the yearly International Guild Conference. The Scavenger Guild, the strongest in America, is led by Thomas Andre. In Japan, the Draw Sword Guild's Goto Ryuji was the country's strongest Hunter, and the Japanese Hunters Association's chairman was Matsumoto Shigeo." },
	{ id = "lore_national", category = "orgs", title = "National Level Hunters", text = "[CANON] Five Hunters survived the fight against the first S-rank Dungeon Break, a dragon on the US West Coast, and became the National Level Hunters: each one worth as much as a country's army. Four are named: Thomas Andre and Christopher Reed of the United States, Liu Zhigang of China, and Siddharth Bachchan of India. The fifth is never named in the original story." },

	-- ── What Happened ──
	{ id = "lore_timeline", category = "history", title = "The Years (relative)", text = "[CANON, years approximate] Year 0: the first Gates. About Year 2: the dragon on the US West Coast, and the National Level Hunters. About Year 6: Jeju Island falls to an S-rank break. Years 7 to 9: raids to take it back fail. About Year 8: Cha Hae-In, Korea's 9th S-rank. Year 10: the story begins; Korea registers a 10th S-rank. After: Jeju retaken, Tokyo's giants, Christopher Reed's death, the chairman's death, the Gate over Seoul, and the war.\n\n[CANON] Canon gives no calendar year for any of it. Here, years are counted from the first Gates." },
	{ id = "lore_jeju", category = "history", title = "Jeju Island", text = "[CANON] About four years before the story began, an S-rank Gate opened on Jeju Island. The raid failed, the Gate broke, and giant ants overran the island; it was abandoned. Three raids to retake it failed. The fourth, a joint Korean and Japanese raid, killed the Ant King. Eight S-ranks died in it." },
	{ id = "lore_tokyo", category = "history", title = "Tokyo", text = "[CANON] Weeks after Jeju, an S-rank Gate opened in Tokyo. Japan hired foreign S-ranks; a barrier that was meant to hold the Gate failed, and giants walked out into the city. A single Hunter from Korea is credited with stopping them." },
	{ id = "lore_war", category = "history", title = "The War", text = "[CANON] At the end of the story, Gates open everywhere at once, and armies come through them, led by beings the Hunters had no name for. Hunters all over the world fight. The war ends in the final battle.", secret = true },
	{ id = "lore_rewrite", category = "history", title = "The Rewritten World", text = "[CANON] After the war, time was turned back about ten years, to before the Gates, and the war was fought out of sight, where nobody on Earth would see it. In the new world the Gates never opened. Nobody remembers.\n\n[GAME] If you let it happen, your life goes on in that world: no Gates, no Hunters, no rank. (Turning your own years back isn't simulated; you go on from where you are.) You can also choose to stay on a world line where the Gates never closed. That line is not canon, and the game says so.", secret = true },

	-- ── Beyond ──
	{ id = "lore_monarchs", category = "beyond", title = "Monarchs", text = "[CANON] Nine Monarchs, each a king of a race of monsters: Ashborn the Shadow Monarch; Antares, Monarch of Destruction, King of Dragons; Querehsha, Queen of Insects; Rakan, King of Beasts; Baran, Monarch of White Flames, King of Demons; Yogumunt, Monarch of Transfiguration; Legia, Monarch of the Beginning, King of Giants; Tarnak, Monarch of Iron Body; and Sillad, the Frost Monarch, King of the Snow Folk.\n\n[GAME] Nobody in your life knows these names. You do, because you're reading the archive.", secret = true },
	{ id = "lore_rulers", category = "beyond", title = "Rulers", text = "[CANON] The Rulers are beings of light, enemies of the Monarchs, who act through human hosts. Their war is older than the Earth's Gates, and the Gates are part of it.", secret = true },

	-- ── how the game works ──
	{ id = "lore_game_modes", category = "game", title = "Two Worlds", text = "[GAME] The Normal World is the story's world: Gates are dangerous and breaks happen, but help usually comes. The High-Danger World is unstable: more Gates open in streets, more break, the Association is stretched thin, help is late or doesn't come, and a bad year can end a life. You pick one on the start screen, for the whole life." },
	{ id = "lore_game_news", category = "game", title = "The News", text = "[GAME] Everything the world does that you're not there for reaches you the way it reaches anyone: the news. Local incidents, national headlines, the world. The canon story's public events are on it, when they happen. Secret things never are." },

	-- ── the bestiary (Hunter/Bestiary.luau): one entry a family ──
	{ id = "lore_beast_ants", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Giant Ants", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: giant ants overran Jeju Island from an S-rank Gate in the story, a canon incident that stays its own. These smaller colonies, in other Gates, are this game's own; there is no Ant King or Queen among them (the Hive Matron boss is ORIGINAL).\n\n[GAME] The size of dogs, then of cars, then bigger. Every Hunter in Korea knows what ants can do to an island. These colonies are smaller. Nobody relaxes.\n\nRanks D to A: Ant Worker, Ant Soldier, Ant Spitter, Winged Ant, Ant Royal Guard.\nWhere: D to A Gates: nests in red sand, tunnels under a dungeon's floor.\nHow they fight: A colony: workers tunnel and swarm, soldiers charge with mandibles, spitters shoot acid, winged ants dive, royal guards plate themselves.\nWeakness: Find the nest's centre. A colony without its guards falls back to protect it." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_aquatic", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: The Deep", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Some Gates open onto a sea. Something always comes up out of it.\n\nRanks D to S: Reef Crab, Merfolk Warrior, Merfolk Tidecaller, Kraken Spawn, Abyssal Serpent.\nWhere: D to S Gates: flooded Gates, black seas inside a dungeon.\nHow they fight: Merfolk with tridents, tidecallers, kraken spawn that lash and ink, and the abyssal serpent.\nWeakness: Out of the water they bring with them, they tire." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_basilisks", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Basilisks", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Many-legged lizards with crests and eyes that make your limbs heavy.\n\nRanks D to S: Cockatrice, Basilisk, Elder Basilisk.\nWhere: D to S Gates: deserts, stone ruins, caves.\nHow they fight: A gaze that stiffens you, a tail that sweeps, spit; the cockatrice is small and vicious.\nWeakness: Don't meet the gaze: step out of its line." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_bears", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Bears", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: ice bears are monsters of the story's Red Gate. These ice bears, cave bears and their alpha are this game's own (cave bears ORIGINAL).\n\n[GAME] White as the snow they come out of, and twice the weight of a car.\n\nRanks C to A: Cave Bear, Ice Bear, Alpha Ice Bear.\nWhere: C to A Gates: caves, frozen worlds, the snowfields of a Red Gate.\nHow they fight: Brutes: a charge, a slam, an alpha's roar; ice bears freeze the air around them.\nWeakness: Their charge runs straight. Step aside." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_beetles", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Beetles", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: the armoured beetles of the mine Gates are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Shells like plate armour, some glowing with the crystal they eat. Their shells sell well, after.\n\nRanks E to C: Scarab, Armoured Beetle, Bombardier Beetle, Rhino Beetle.\nWhere: E to C Gates: mines, deserts of red sand, crystal caves.\nHow they fight: Armoured brutes that charge, scarabs that swarm and burrow, bombardiers that spray scalding liquid.\nWeakness: The joints under the shell, and the belly." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_boars", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Boars", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Tusks, bristles, a bad temper. Farm Gates leak boars more than anything else.\n\nRanks E to C: Wild Boar, Razorback, Ironhide Boar.\nWhere: E to C Gates: forests, farmland Gates, hill country.\nHow they fight: Charges. Razorbacks go berserk when hurt; ironhide boars wear plate.\nWeakness: A boar that has charged has to turn around." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_centaurs", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Centaurs", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Half horse, half rider, all hunter. They keep their distance until they don't.\n\nRanks C to A: Centaur Skirmisher, Centaur Archer, Centaur Lancer.\nWhere: C to A Gates: plains under a second sky.\nHow they fight: Skirmishers throw javelins, archers volley, lancers charge.\nWeakness: Close the distance; a centaur can't turn in a narrow street." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_centipedes", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Centipedes and Wyrms", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own (the System's penalty-zone centipedes are not these).\n\n[GAME] Hundreds of legs, or none at all. You hear them before you see them, if you're listening.\n\nRanks D to S: Tunnel Centipede, Ironback Centipede, Sand Wyrm.\nWhere: D to S Gates: tunnels, deserts, the sand seas of the deepest Gates.\nHow they fight: Burrowers: under the ground, then up beneath you. The sand wyrm splits the street with a quake.\nWeakness: Keep moving. They come up where you were standing." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_constructs", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Constructs and Mimics", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Things made to guard something. The treasure in a Gate's vault is sometimes the trap.\n\nRanks D to A: Living Armour, Mimic, Gargoyle, Rune Sentinel.\nWhere: D to A Gates: vaults, treasure rooms, guarded halls.\nHow they fight: Living armour guards, mimics wait, gargoyles dive, rune sentinels gaze.\nWeakness: A mimic is a chest. Check chests." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_dark_elves", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Dark Elves", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: dark elves are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Dusky grey skin, white hair, violet eyes. They keep giant spiders the way people keep dogs.\n\nRanks B to S: Dark Elf Stalker, Dark Elf Arbalest, Dark Elf Spellblade, Dark Elf Sorceress, Dark Elf Matriarch.\nWhere: B to S Gates: lightless caverns, spider-haunted temples.\nHow they fight: Assassins and sorcerers. Stalkers step out of shadows behind you, arbalests shoot from the dark, sorceresses drain the life out of you, the matriarch calls her spiders.\nWeakness: Light. Flood the room and they lose half their tricks." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_demons", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Demons", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: demons are monsters of a demon dungeon in the story. These soldiers, knights, warlocks and nobles are this game's own designs; no canon demon lord or gatekeeper is among them.\n\n[GAME] Horns, burning eyes, iron and fire. Demons fight like an army, because that's what they are.\n\nRanks E to S: Imp, Demon Soldier, Demon Knight, Demon Warlock, Demon Noble.\nWhere: E to S Gates: castles of black stone, burning plains.\nHow they fight: Imps throw fire, soldiers cleave, knights burn, warlocks drop meteors, a noble commands them all.\nWeakness: Their pride: a demon noble fights its own battle and lets its soldiers die." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_dragonkin", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Dragon-kin", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: dragons are the race of the Monarch of Destruction. These dragon-kin are lesser, and this game's own; no named dragon is among them.\n\n[GAME] Black scales, burning eyes. Dragon-kin are what the Association means when it says a Gate 'reads S'.\n\nRanks A to S: Dragon-kin Drakeling, Dragon-kin, Dragon-kin Knight, Dragon-kin Flamecaller.\nWhere: A and S Gates: volcanic Gates, the deepest S-rank dungeons.\nHow they fight: Fire and armour: breath that melts plate, tail sweeps, knights in black scale, flamecallers who drop meteors.\nWeakness: None worth counting on. Bring more Hunters." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_drakes", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Wyverns and Drakes", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: wyverns appear in the story. These wyverns, hatchlings, fire drakes and storm wyverns are this game's own (the drakes ORIGINAL).\n\n[GAME] Two legs and two wings: a wyvern is not a dragon. It doesn't need to be.\n\nRanks C to S: Wyvern Hatchling, Fire Drake, Wyvern, Storm Wyvern.\nWhere: C to S Gates: mountain peaks, volcanic Gates, storm skies.\nHow they fight: Fliers and brutes: dives, stinging tails, fire and storm.\nWeakness: Their wings. Ground one and it's half the threat." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_dwarves", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Dwarves", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: dwarves are not in Solo Leveling. This game's dwarves are the silent miners of the deep crystal Gates.\n\n[GAME] Short, broad, braided beards, iron helmets. They look like dwarves out of a storybook, but they never speak, and they defend a crystal seam to the last.\n\nRanks D to A: Dwarf Miner, Dwarf Axe-guard, Dwarf Thunderer, Dwarf Runesmith, Dwarf Berserker, Dwarf Forge-lord.\nWhere: D to A Gates: deep mines, rune-carved halls, forges that never go out.\nHow they fight: Miners burrow, axe-guards hold a shield wall, thunderers throw blasting charges, runesmiths ward the line, a forge-lord leads with a hammer that sets the ground on fire.\nWeakness: The shield wall turns: hit it from the side." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_elementals", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Elementals", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Mana that took a shape: a wisp, a fire, a block of living ice, a storm with eyes.\n\nRanks E to S: Mana Wisp, Earth Elemental, Fire Elemental, Frost Elemental, Storm Elemental, Inferno Elemental.\nWhere: E to S Gates: mana-saturated caverns, volcanic, frozen and storm Gates.\nHow they fight: Casters: bolts, novas, lightning, meteors, with their element circling them.\nWeakness: The core inside the element. Hit that, not the fire." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_forest_elves", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Forest Elves", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: forest elves are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Green leather, long ears, longer bows. Nobody knows if they are defending their forest or only their Gate. They don't talk to Hunters.\n\nRanks D to B: Forest Elf Scout, Forest Elf Warden, Forest Elf Ranger, Forest Elf Druid.\nWhere: D to B Gates: dark forests under a second sky, overgrown ruins.\nHow they fight: Rangers and wardens. Archers volley from the trees, wardens close with twin blades, druids heal them and call up vines.\nWeakness: Fire, and the open. Away from the trees they are only very good archers." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_giants", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Giants", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: giants come out of the story's S-rank Gate in Tokyo, a canon incident that stays its own. These hill, stone and frost giants are this game's own, from other Gates.\n\n[GAME] A giant is a city-block problem. When one comes out of a Gate, the Association closes the district before it asks anything else.\n\nRanks B to S: Young Hill Giant, Hill Giant, Stone Giant, Frost Giant, Frost Giant Jarl.\nWhere: B to S Gates: mountainsides, frozen plains, the biggest caverns a dungeon has.\nHow they fight: Brutes the size of buildings. They stomp, throw boulders, and the frost giants freeze the ground around them.\nWeakness: Their size. A giant can't see what's at its ankles, and it turns slowly." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_goblins", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Goblins", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: goblins fill the low-rank Gates of the story's first raids. These tribes, and their shamans, riders and warbosses, are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Small, green, quick and spiteful. Alone, a goblin is something a careful E-rank handles with a pipe. They are never alone.\n\nRanks E to D: Goblin, Goblin Archer, Goblin Shaman, Hobgoblin, Goblin Wolf Rider, Goblin Warboss.\nWhere: E and D Gates: warrens, limestone caves, the rotted timber forts they build inside a dungeon.\nHow they fight: A pack: grunts in front, an archer or two behind, a shaman keeping the rest alive, a warboss shouting at all of them.\nWeakness: Kill the shaman first. A goblin pack without its healer breaks after the first two go down." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_golems", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Golems", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: golems are Gate monsters in the story. These mud, iron, crystal, stone and magma golems and the colossus are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Nobody knows who builds golems inside a Gate. They guard something. They keep guarding it after it's gone.\n\nRanks E to S: Mud Golem, Iron Golem, Crystal Golem, Stone Golem, Magma Golem, Colossus.\nWhere: E to S Gates: ruins, mines, the halls of whatever built them.\nHow they fight: Brutes: slams, thrown boulders, quakes, and a stony guard that nothing gets through.\nWeakness: The glowing core. Everything else is just stone." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_great_cats", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Great Cats", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Black panthers you can't see at dusk, sabre cats, and tigers that crackle with storm.\n\nRanks C to A: Dusk Panther, Sabre Cat, Thunder Tiger.\nWhere: C to A Gates: jungles, storm plains, dusk forests.\nHow they fight: Stalkers: a pounce from cover, a sabre bite, a thunder tiger's lightning.\nWeakness: They hunt alone. Stand together." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_harpies", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Harpies and Birds", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Feathered wings, talons, and a scream you feel in your teeth.\n\nRanks D to A: Gloom Crow, Harpy, Harpy Shrieker, Thunderbird.\nWhere: D to A Gates: cliffs, ruined towers, storm skies.\nHow they fight: Fliers: dives from above, shrieks that shake you, a thunderbird's lightning.\nWeakness: Ranged attacks, and a roof." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_high_orcs", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: High Orcs", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: high orcs, and high orc shamans, are A-rank Gate monsters in the story. These warriors and their warlord are this game's own; no canon individual is among them.\n\n[GAME] Taller than orcs, darker, armoured in iron, painted for war. They plan. They retreat to draw you in. Many raid teams have learned that the hard way.\n\nRanks B to A: High Orc, High Orc Berserker, High Orc Shaman, High Orc Warlord.\nWhere: B and A Gates: fortress dungeons, war-camps under a red sky.\nHow they fight: Elite war bands. Shamans call lightning and ward their warriors; berserkers charge; the warlord's war cry makes all of them faster and harder to stop.\nWeakness: Their shamans. A high orc band with its shaman down is still deadly, but it can be outlasted." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_hounds", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Hell-hounds", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's many-headed hounds are not the Demon Castle's gatekeeper.\n\n[GAME] Black hounds with embers in their fur. The big ones have two heads, or three.\n\nRanks D to S: Ember Hound, Two-headed Hound, Three-headed Warhound.\nWhere: D to S Gates: burning plains, demon castles' outer walls.\nHow they fight: Fire breath, leaps, howls; the more heads, the more fire.\nWeakness: Every head breathes, but there is only one body." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_ice_elves", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Ice Elves", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: the ice elves (the Snow Folk) are the monsters of the story's Red Gate. These hunters, mages and their warchief are this game's own; the Red Gate's boss is not among them.\n\n[GAME] Pale as snow, tall and slender, long ears, eyes like ice in sunlight. Ice elves hunt Hunters the way Hunters hunt monsters: patiently, and for food.\n\nRanks B to S: Ice Elf Scout, Ice Elf Hunter, Ice Elf Archer, Ice Elf Frost-mage, Ice Elf Priestess, Ice Elf Warchief.\nWhere: B to S Gates: frozen worlds, snowfields under a white sun, the frozen Gates that turn red.\nHow they fight: Hunters. Scouts come fast and vanish, archers rain frost arrows, frost-mages freeze the ground, priestesses ward and heal, the warchief leads the hunt.\nWeakness: They fight as one hunt. Break their line of sight to each other and the hunt falls apart." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_kobolds", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Kobolds", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: kobolds are not in Solo Leveling. This game's kobolds are small reptile-headed miners of the crystal Gates.\n\n[GAME] Waist-high, rust-scaled, horned like little goats. Kobolds strip a Gate's crystal faster than any mining crew, and they defend a seam as if it were their young.\n\nRanks E to D: Kobold, Kobold Slinger, Kobold Tunneler, Kobold Trapper, Kobold Foreman.\nWhere: E and D Gates: crystal-lined mines and the tunnels under them.\nHow they fight: A swarm that digs. Tunnelers come up under you, slingers and trappers throw from the tunnel mouths, a foreman with a whip keeps them at it.\nWeakness: They panic in the open. Pull them out of the tunnels and they scatter." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_lizardfolk", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Lizardmen", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: lizardmen are a Gate monster in the story. These tribes, their shamans, croc-headed guards and bog toads are this game's own (the toads ORIGINAL).\n\n[GAME] Scaled, upright, with tails that hit as hard as their spears. They fight in the water if they can.\n\nRanks D to B: Lizardman Skirmisher, Bog Toad, Lizardman, Lizardman Shaman, Croc-headed Guard.\nWhere: D to B Gates: swamps that smell of copper, flooded ruins.\nHow they fight: Skirmishers throw javelins, warriors sweep with tail and spear, shamans spit poison and heal, croc-headed guards hold the line; the tribes keep bog toads.\nWeakness: The cold. Lizardmen slow down when it's cold." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_lycans", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Lycans", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL type: this game's lycans are not the System's steel-fanged ones.\n\n[GAME] A wolf that learned to stand. Lycans hunt in packs, and the packmaster remembers faces.\n\nRanks B to A: Ashen Lycan, Lycan Packmaster.\nWhere: B and A Gates: moonlit forests, ruined villages under a second sky.\nHow they fight: Wolf-headed hunters on two legs: a leap, a howl, and a packmaster who frenzies when hurt.\nWeakness: Whatever the old stories say about silver, they bleed like anything else. Outlast the frenzy." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_mantises", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Mantises", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Folded scythes, triangular heads, and absolutely no hurry until they move.\n\nRanks C to A: Blade Mantis, Ghost Mantis, Reaper Mantis.\nWhere: C to A Gates: overgrown ruins, jungles under a green sky.\nHow they fight: Ambush hunters: still, then not. They leap, cut, and the ghost mantis steps out of nowhere.\nWeakness: Patience. A mantis that misses its first strike is exposed." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_minotaurs", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Minotaurs", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Bull-headed, axe-handed, and at home in a maze you are not.\n\nRanks B to S: Minotaur Raider, Minotaur, Minotaur Champion.\nWhere: B to S Gates: labyrinths, maze dungeons of black stone.\nHow they fight: Charges, axes, stomps; the champion's war cry.\nWeakness: The charge. A minotaur that misses hits the wall." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_naga", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Naga", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: naga appear among the story's Gate monsters. These sirens, four-armed myrmidons and tidecallers are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] A body like a person's above, a serpent's below, a cobra's hood around the head. They sing to each other in the dark.\n\nRanks C to A: Naga, Naga Siren, Naga Myrmidon, Naga Tidecaller.\nWhere: C to A Gates: flooded temples, sunken ruins, black-water caverns.\nHow they fight: A naga's tail is as dangerous as its spear. Sirens shriek and heal, myrmidons fight with four blades, a tidecaller leads.\nWeakness: Get off the water's edge. On dry stone they are slower than they look." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_ogres", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Ogres", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: ogres are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Fat, enormous, and stronger than they look, which is very strong. An ogre hits like a falling wall.\n\nRanks B to A: Ogre, Ogre Mage, Ogre Warlord.\nWhere: B and A Gates: caverns, hill forts, the ruins of something older than them.\nHow they fight: Brutes. They slam the ground, hurl rocks, and the two-headed ogre mages freeze whatever their kin miss.\nWeakness: Slow. Fight them where they can't reach you and they have only the rocks." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_orcs", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Orcs", text = "[CANON, type] Canon type: orcs are a common middle-rank Gate monster. These war bands and their shamans and berserkers are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Big, green-grey, tusked, and they fight as a band. A C-rank team that treats an orc war band like a goblin pack doesn't come home.\n\nRanks D to B: Orc Scout, Orc, Orc Archer, Orc Shaman, Orc Berserker, Orc Warchief.\nWhere: C and B Gates: war camps, black stone mazes, the forts of a dungeon's second sky.\nHow they fight: A war band: axe-armed warriors, archers on the flanks, a shaman throwing fire and patching wounds, berserkers who stop feeling pain, a warchief in front.\nWeakness: Discipline. Orcs charge; a held line and a Tanker's provoke break the charge before it lands." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_plants", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Living Plants", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] The forest inside a Gate is alive in a way the forest outside isn't.\n\nRanks E to A: Mandrake, Vine Lasher, Spore Shambler, Treant, Elder Treant.\nWhere: E to A Gates: dark forests, overgrown temples.\nHow they fight: Mandrakes scream, vine lashers whip, spore shamblers poison the air, treants stomp and quake.\nWeakness: Fire, of course." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_scorpions", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Scorpions", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] The tail comes over the back faster than you expect, every time.\n\nRanks D to A: Scorpling, Sand Scorpion, Emperor Scorpion.\nWhere: D to A Gates: red deserts, sun-baked ruins.\nHow they fight: Pincers to hold, a tail to sting. The emperor scorpion spits venom and shells up.\nWeakness: Behind it. A scorpion turns slowly." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_slimes", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Slimes", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] A wobbling ball of mana-jelly with a bright core. Children's cartoons make them cute. They are not cute.\n\nRanks E to B: Mana Slime, Acid Slime, Magma Slime, Crowned Slime.\nWhere: E to B Gates: caves, sewers, crystal pools.\nHow they fight: They split when hurt, spit acid, burn; a crowned slime calls more of them.\nWeakness: The nucleus. Pop that and the jelly is just jelly." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_spiders", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Spiders", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Eight legs, eight eyes, and a mark on the back that glows when it's hungry.\n\nRanks E to B: Spiderling, Giant Spider, Weaver Spider, Phase Spider.\nWhere: E to B Gates: webbed caves, dark forests, temples nobody tends.\nHow they fight: Webs, ambushes, swarms of spiderlings; weavers call more of them; phase spiders blink across a room.\nWeakness: Fire, and cutting the web before you walk into it." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_trolls", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Trolls", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: trolls are this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Long-armed, hunched, long-nosed. Trolls are not clever and do not need to be: they get back up.\n\nRanks D to B: Moss Troll, Cave Troll, Frost Troll, Troll Elder.\nWhere: D to A Gates: caves, bogs, the frozen Gates (frost trolls).\nHow they fight: Brutes that heal. A troll left alone for a few seconds closes its own wounds.\nWeakness: Keep hitting it. A troll only mends when nothing has hurt it for a moment." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_undead", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: The Restless Dead", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL, apart from the undead-like knights (a canon type: armoured knights that serve in a dungeon). Skeletons, wraiths, liches and the Hollow King are this game's own; never shadow soldiers.\n\n[GAME] Bones and cold light. Whatever kingdom these were, it was inside the Gate, and it ended a long time ago.\n\nRanks E to S: Skeleton Soldier, Skeleton Archer, Skeleton Mage, Ghoul, Wraith, Grave Knight, Lich, Hollow King, Bone Drake.\nWhere: E to S Gates: crypts, sunken archives, throne rooms of empty kingdoms.\nHow they fight: Skeletons with shields and bows, mages, gaunt ghouls, wraiths that drain, grave knights, and liches that raise more of them.\nWeakness: Light, and breaking whatever holds them together." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_vermin", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Cave Vermin", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: the rats, bats and crawlers of low-rank cave Gates.\n\n[GAME] Every Gate has its vermin: things that live on what the bigger monsters leave. Raid teams kill them by the hundred and nobody counts.\n\nRanks E to D: Giant Rat, Cave Bat, Cave Crawler, Shriek Bat.\nWhere: E and D Gates: caves, sewers, the dark corners of every dungeon.\nHow they fight: Swarms. Bats drop from the ceiling, rats come in waves, crawlers jump.\nWeakness: Light and noise. Most of them are only brave in the dark." },
	{ id = "lore_beast_wolves", category = BESTIARY, title = "Bestiary: Wolves", text = "[GAME] ORIGINAL: this game's own.\n\n[GAME] Grey wolves, dire wolves, frost wolves: the same hunt, at different sizes.\n\nRanks E to C: Grey Wolf, Dire Wolf, Frost Wolf, Wolf Alpha.\nWhere: E to C Gates: forests, snowfields, the plains of a second sky.\nHow they fight: A pack: fast, flanking, leaping. The alpha's howl drives them; frost wolves breathe ice.\nWeakness: The alpha. Kill it and the pack hesitates." },
}
